Res-Q — how to work in this folder
==================================

Standing instructions for any AI agent (or person) picking this repository up. Read this
before doing anything else: it is the difference between a session that needs the same
brief typed out again and one that just works. Last updated 2026-10-12.

The two standing rules
----------------------

1. Always keep a visible todo list. Every request, however small, gets one; mark each item
   done as it lands rather than at the end. The user reads it as live progress.

2. Everything you change goes live by itself, and you must prove it. Nothing is left
   uncommitted, and "I pushed it" is not the finish line — "the live site is serving this
   commit" is. See *Verifying a change is live*, below.

How "goes live" is wired
------------------------

    edit in this folder
      → scripts/autosync.sh commits it (once the edit has settled) and pushes main
      → .github/workflows/deploy.yml asks Render to rebuild that commit
      → the job waits for https://res-q-api.onrender.com/api/health to report the new
        revision, and goes red if it never does

* The Render service serves **both** the pages and the API from one process, so a page
  fix and an API fix ship together. `render.yaml` → `rootDir: backend`; the document root
  is `frontend/`. There is no separate frontend host to poke.
* `scripts/autosync.sh` runs as a launchd job (`com.resq.autosync`) that starts at login
  and restarts if it dies. It publishes anything in `frontend/`, `backend/`, `supabase/`,
  docs and config; it never force-pushes and never commits through a half-finished merge.
* It holds an edit for ~10 seconds of stillness first, and it refuses to commit a backend
  that does not compile. So a broken change waits for you, and a finished one leaves on
  its own.

Useful commands:

    scripts/autosync.sh --status      # is it running, and the last few things it did
    scripts/autosync.sh --once        # do a pass now, in the foreground
    scripts/autosync.sh --install     # (re)install the launchd job after moving the repo
    scripts/autosync.sh --uninstall   # stop it
    tail -f .git/autosync.log         # what it has been doing
    touch .git/autosync-off           # pause publishing; rm it to resume
    rm .git/autosync-off

Verifying a change is live
--------------------------

    git rev-parse --short=12 HEAD
    curl -s https://res-q-api.onrender.com/api/health

`/api/health` carries `revision` — the commit the answering process was built from (the
host's `RENDER_GIT_COMMIT`, or the working copy when run locally). Those two shas must
match. If they do not:

    gh run list --limit 3            # did the deploy workflow run, and what did it say
    gh secret list                   # is RENDER_DEPLOY_HOOK_URL (or RENDER_API_KEY) set
    scripts/autosync.sh --once       # is the push itself still sitting here

A stale revision means a deploy that never happened, **not** a code bug — check the deploy
chain before touching the code. This has already cost one debugging session: three commits
including the donor dashboard's route map were pushed and correct while the live site kept
serving a build from days earlier, which looked exactly like "the map is broken".

Deploy secrets
--------------

The workflow needs one of these, set as a GitHub Actions secret on this repository:

* `RENDER_DEPLOY_HOOK_URL` — Render → the service → Settings → Deploy Hook. Simplest.
* `RENDER_API_KEY` + `RENDER_SERVICE_ID` — deploys a named commit and can be polled.

Set them **from a file**, so the value never appears in a transcript:

    gh secret set RENDER_DEPLOY_HOOK_URL < /path/to/hook.txt

Never commit a hook URL, an API key or a service id. `render.yaml` deliberately uses
`sync: false` for the values that are somebody's own (origins, Supabase, the model key).

Running it locally
------------------

    python3 backend/server.py            # pages + API on http://127.0.0.1:8080
    python3 backend/server.py 8123       # or on a port of your choosing

Check the port is free first (`lsof -i :8080`) — several agents share this checkout and a
second server on the same port looks like a site that will not load. Demo accounts, all
with the password `ResqDemo1!`:

    luis@resq.test    Ortega Deli, the Miami donor (the demo's subject)
    rosa@resq.test    dana@resq.test    (the other test donors)

Pages are plain files under `frontend/`; the donor dashboard is
`frontend/res_q_dashboard_donor.html`. A page reaches the API through `apiUrl()` in
`frontend/resq_api.js`, whose base is the deployed Render origin by default — open the
pages from `python3 backend/server.py` and the calls go to Render unless the page sets
`window.RESQ_API_BASE` first. `RESQ_PERSIST=off` (the default, and what the deployment
runs) keeps accounts, sessions and orders in the worker's memory.

Project conventions
-------------------

* Python 3 standard library only — no framework, no ORM. Routes live in
  `backend/server.py`; every constant and threshold lives in `backend/config.py`.
* Comments carry the *why*, at length, in full sentences. Match that voice; do not add
  banner comments or restate what the next line says.
* Frontend is single-file HTML with Tailwind from a CDN and one inline `<script>` — there
  is no build step and no bundler, so a change is a file edit.
* Long-lived facts about the deployment (origins, modes, the render.yaml contract) belong
  in `README.md`; this file is the short version for whoever is about to start work.

